 Android Utility Function Library
==================================

If you need a feature that is native to Linux but not present on other
platforms, construct a platform-dependent implementation that shares
the Linux interface. That way the actual device runs as "light" as
possible.

If that isn't feasible, create a system-independent interface and hide
the details.

The ultimate goal is *not* to create a super-duper platform abstraction
layer. The goal is to provide an optimized solution for Linux with
reasonable implementations for other platforms.


 Run-time resource overlay
===========================


 Description
-------------

Android resources (integers, strings, images, etc, as specified in the
res/, assets/ and raw/ source directories) are bundled as part of .apk
packages. Application access to resources are routed through the
AssetManager.

Run-time overlay allows the AssetManager to load a designated overlay
package alongside the original package. During resource lookup, the
application will be returned the corresponding resource of the overlay
package, if present.

Run-time overlay allows for

- package customization decoupled from package development
- rapid prototyping of libraries costly to rebuild (notably
  framework-res.apk).
- debugging

The use of run-time overlay is optional and transparent to the targeted
package; its run-time cost is negligible, its memory cost only that of the
extra package loaded. Construction of overlay packages is decoupled from that
of target packages, the only link between the two is the type and symbolic name
of the resources to overlay.

While technically possible to override any resource in any application,
run-time overlay should be applied judiciously. One step towards this is
to store overlay packages in the /system partition, barring end-users
from applying run-time overlay on arbitrary packages.


 Resource lookup priorities
----------------------------

A resource may have alternative values depending on phone language, current
screen orientation, etc. Overlay resources may come in alternative forms as
well, with one exception: where a resource must have a default value, an overlay
resource does not. This means it is possible to overlay eg a string resource for
a specific language without providing an overlay default.

The priority order when looking for a resource is as follows, where `target' is
the package overlaid, `overlay' is the overlay package, and X is a configuration
matching the phone's current configuration better than the default one:

- overlay/values-X
- target/values-X
- overlay/values
- target/values


 Usage
-------

Overlay packages are compiled like regular packages based on Android.mk.
Resources are provided as usual; overlay packages lack source code.

To match target and overlay packages, some naming conventions must be
followed. To create an overlay package targeting a package "p.apk" with
package name "a.b.c", ie compiled with the following options:

- AndroidManifest.xml : <manifest ... package="a.b.c">
- Android.mk          : LOCAL_PACKAGE_NAME := p

create the overlay package as usual, while setting the package name and
output path as follows, and instructing aapt to mark the package as an
overlay package:

- AndroidManifest.xml : <manifest ... package="a.b.c.overlay">
- Android.mk          : LOCAL_PACKAGE_NAME := overlay-p
                        LOCAL_MODULE_PATH := $(TARGET_OUT)/overlay
                        LOCAL_AAPT_FLAGS := -o

Note: the aapt flag -o denotes an overlay package targeting a regular
application package. Should framework-res.apk be targeted, use the -O
(capital oh) flag instead.


 Examples
----------

For a working example, see frameworks/base/core/tests/coretests,
subdirectories

- src/android/overlay (targeted package)
- apks/install_overlay (overlay package)
